# Secrets — nothing here should ever exist, but belt and braces
.env
.env.*
*.key
*.pem
*.pfx
*_secret*
*_token*


# =============================================================================
#  TEMPORARY: UNITY TERRAIN EXCLUDED DURING DEVELOPMENT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  WHY: TerrainData is a large binary blob rewritten in full on every save.
#       Move one tree and Git stores a whole new copy, permanently. Committing
#       through an active sculpting phase would eat the 10 GiB LFS quota.
#
#  WARNING: while this block is active, the island is NOT backed up by Git.
#       Keep a copy of Assets/_Project/Art/Terrain/ on a cloud drive or an
#       external disk. If this machine dies today, the island dies with it.
#
#  NAMING CONVENTION — these patterns only work if you follow it:
#       Store terrain assets in  Assets/_Project/Art/Terrain/
#       Name them               Aanniarvik_Terrain.asset
#       Unity's default name is "New Terrain.asset" — rename it immediately.
#       TerrainData can be safely renamed from inside the editor.
#
#  TO RE-INCLUDE WHEN THE LAYOUT IS LOCKED:
#       1. Delete this entire block.
#       2. Confirm the LFS rules in .gitattributes cover the terrain patterns.
#       3. git add Assets/_Project/Art/Terrain/
#       4. git status  — verify the .asset shows as an LFS pointer, not raw text
#       5. Commit once. Batch future terrain commits at milestones, not daily.
# =============================================================================
*_[Tt]errain.asset
*_[Tt]errain.asset.meta
*[Tt]errain[Dd]ata.asset
*[Tt]errain[Dd]ata.asset.meta
New Terrain.asset
New Terrain.asset.meta
/[Aa]ssets/_Project/Art/Terrain/*.asset
/[Aa]ssets/_Project/Art/Terrain/*.asset.meta

# Heightmap exports — Terrain Settings > Export Raw.
# UNCOMMENT the ignore below if you don't want these tracked. Leaving them
# TRACKED is recommended: a .raw heightmap is ~2 MB and is a cheap partial
# backup of the island's shape while the TerrainData itself is excluded.
# It does NOT capture splatmaps, detail layers or painted tree instances.
# *.raw
# *.r16


# =============================================================================
#  BAKED LIGHTING — review before your first bake
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  LightingData.asset is binary, large, and regenerated on every bake. Unlike
#  terrain it is derived data: you can always rebake. Ignoring it costs you
#  bake time on a fresh clone and nothing else.
#
#  Unlike TerrainData, LightingData CANNOT be safely renamed — Unity keeps it
#  in a folder named after its scene, so the path is the only handle you have.
#
#  Keep these commented while lighting is unbaked. Uncomment before the first
#  real bake if the file turns out to be large.
# =============================================================================
# /[Aa]ssets/**/LightingData.asset
# /[Aa]ssets/**/LightingData.asset.meta
# /[Aa]ssets/**/ReflectionProbe-*.exr
# /[Aa]ssets/**/Lightmap-*.exr

# =============================================================================
#  THIRD-PARTY / ASSET STORE PACKAGES — Assets/ThirdParty/
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  WHY: per Docs/unity-conventions.md, Assets/ThirdParty/ is where imported,
#       never-edited third-party packages live (2026-08-23: Unity's official
#       "Terrain Sample Asset Pack" — brushes, sample textures, vegetation
#       models). These are large, freely re-downloadable from the Asset Store,
#       and not authored content, so there's no reason to version them.
#
#  WARNING: this also ignores the .meta files, which carry GUIDs. If anything
#       else in the project ends up referencing an asset from here by GUID
#       (a material, a prefab, a Vegetation Spawner entry), a fresh clone that
#       re-imports the package will get DIFFERENT GUIDs and that reference
#       will silently break. Fine for "sculpting reference" use. NOT fine once
#       something from in here is actually shipping in the game — pull that
#       specific file out into Assets/_Project/Art/ (with proper attribution/
#       license notes) before it's load-bearing, same as any other asset.
# =============================================================================
/[Aa]ssets/ThirdParty/**
!/[Aa]ssets/ThirdParty/.gitkeep